#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/content-change-review.bpmn:21
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Getting a change to a folio's CONTENT from a request into a published rendering: the author says what they want, an agent works out its scope and makes it, a review committee accepts or refuses it, and the pipeline deploys. You are in this process when what is changing is a folio — a chapter, a block, a piece of deterministic logic — rather than the platform that renders it. `code-change-review` is the same shape for the platform: a content change is accepted by a REVIEW COMMITTEE on editorial grounds, a platform change by a code reviewer on engineering ones. Two things this is not. It is not the feedback workflow: `todo-review` triages what readers said about content that already exists. And the rendering the author reviews is a STAGED one, because a person cannot assess a rendered artefact from a description of it. The diagram shows the phases and the hand-offs between them; each phase is its own process. Making the change is content-change-review-make, staging it is content-change-review-stage, the committee's review is content-change-review-committee, and merging and deploying is content-change-review-publish. This follows bpmn-authoring's rule of one phase per call activity."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/content-change-review.bpmn:135
#. Diagram label
msgid "Iterate on author feedback"
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/content-change-review.bpmn:30
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Judges the staged rendering against what was asked for and either sends it back for another iteration or submits it onward. Unlike editing-hci-validation's equivalent lane, acceptance itself is not this lane's call — that is the committee's, inside Call_CommitteeReview."
msgstr ""

